you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news  on today's programme the millions of  homes at risk because of extreme weather  events made worse through climate change  our more money for clean energy in  developing countries could help slow  global warming  [Music]  and the campaigner who took on big banks  over their financing of coal projects  and won  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those developing the solutions  coming up the simple switch  which could save more than a million  tons of co2  but first more than eight million homes  could be lost every single year around  the world  because of extreme weather events made  more likely because of climate change  research from the charity shelter box at  northumbria university found that if  these events continue to increase at  existing rates  167 million homes could be lost  by 2040. that's the equivalent of every  home  in the uk being wiped out six times over  and looking specifically at the uk  according to the government five  million properties are at risk of  flooding from rivers  or the sea with me now is sam  stricanthan chief executive of  shelterbox hello to you sad thanks very  much for being  with us i mean what those numbers do is  set out in the clearest terms the human  cost but when you think of climate  change  it's not always the first thing that  springs to mind  that's right sarah it's often we focus  on the natural world and of course  that's important and  animals are also helpless but the  reality is that we're forgetting that  the impact of climate change has already  arrived for many human beings and  communities around the world  and that's where shelter box has been  working after floods  droughts and so many other  weather-related emergencies that  are human-centered they're not natural  disasters they're human-created  disasters the scale  of the impact of this it's often hard  for us to get our head around isn't it  let's look at on an individual level at  that human cost and some of the people  that you've had to help  yes the tragedy is that we can lose  sight of individual stories in the  numbers when especially when you get  into the millions for example  um one lady whose story still sticks  with me is sagarika  in india who was  devastated her family lost their home  after cyclone unfunded last year  and that reached winds of 185 million  uh sorry 185 miles an hour and it meant  that not only did she lose her home  she lost two bags of rice that they'd  collected that was soaked  and they lost their livelihood and  thanks to us at least they were able to  have emergency shelter but not only did  she  get that she also had the need for  permanent disaster resilient housing  which was simply  something that the world is not paying  enough attention about  the other issue is that she's been  living there 25 years  and year on year she has seen the  weather worsening and this is not normal  this is not natural this is being caused  by  climate change and it's tied to our  emissions and our  choices we make so sagarika's story is a  tragedy we were  able to help her in the short term her  long-term future is really dependent  on the choices and actions we make to  provide  better futures and lower emissions all  around the world  plenty for leaders to think about with  the g7 summit and cop 26 coming up sanj  thanks very much indeed  thank you in today's other climate news  world leaders are arriving this evening  for the g7 summit in cornwall  the event promised to be the first  carbon neutral g7 meeting will be  attended by leaders from canada  france germany italy japan the uk  and the us the group of seven will  discuss ways for their countries to  tackle climate change  a large oil slick from the fire damage  container ship that sunk off the coast  of sri lanka  has been photographed for the first time  satellite images show evidence of oil  seeping from the wrecked vessel  increasing fears of the environmental  impact of the disaster the ship was  carrying hundreds of barrels of  chemicals  as well as plastic pellets when it  caught fire last month  the oil and gas company shell will  accelerate its plans to cut  carbon emissions the announcement  follows a ruling by a dutch court last  month  that found the company had insufficient  plans to meet climate goals  chief executive ben van burden said that  the oil giant will rise to the challenge  but added he felt disappointed that  shell was singled out  the uk's first fully electric coach  dubbed the carbon battle bus  has been launched today it marks the  start of a zero zero-carbon tour  across the uk which aims to showcase  climate-friendly initiatives  and sustainable journeys the trip will  end at the cop 26 climate summit in  glasgow in november  halogen light bulb sales will end this  september  as part of the uk's wider efforts to  tackle climate change  halogen bulbs are far more energy  intensive than led alternatives  which last five times longer and produce  the same amount of light  but use up to 80 percent less power  the shift is expected to cut co2  emissions by 1.26 million tons  equivalent to removing half a million  cars from uk roads  and landowners are being offered grants  to create woodlands  the 15.9 million pound england woodland  creation offer  can be used to cover all the costs of  planting trees and maintaining woodlands  for 10 years the scheme aims to create  woodlands with a range of tree species  to help store carbon and tackle climate  change with extra money allocated for  woods that the public can access  let's take you over to our data  dashboard now and look at the real-time  power mix  in great britain at the moment you can  see that 29  of electricity is coming from fossil  fuels 49  from renewable sources and 22 percent  from nuclear  and there's been huge investment in  clean energy in the uk and other  developed countries  in recent years that isn't the case  everywhere  a new report from the international  energy agency says the world's climate  future  depends on developing economies  transitioning to cleaner energy  and the investment to help them do so  should be a top priority  emerging and developing economies  account for two-thirds of the world's  population  but have only a fifth of global  investment in clean energy  they currently heavily rely on fossil  fuels for their power  if you have a look at the bottom of the  chart you can see that last year the  vast majority of energy in emerging and  developing economies  was produced by coal oil or gas  in 2020 less than 150 billion us dollars  were spent on clean energy investment in  these countries  but for the world to reach its net zero  targets the iea says  this figure needs to increase by a  factor of seven to more than a trillion  dollars by  2030. let's bring in tony tier ceo of  renewables africa who work with  governments  and businesses to install solar capacity  hello to you tony  a big investment in the uk in renewables  but how easy is it to access that  investment in the places where you work  well i think it's quite difficult to  access those investments because  most of the time what we see is the  investment goes into major economies but  when it comes to an emerging market  it's not that easy to get it for simple  reason that that investment  i would say investors are not maybe  attracted enough by emerging markets  because of the perceived risk regarding  the uh currency risk or maybe the uh  polit political stability so that's why  we are seeing some issues with regard to  emerging markets and specifically africa  so not just financial hurdles but  political ones as well  uh yes not financial but also political  and i would add as well  regulatory because you need to have an  environment that is conducive for  for renewables to happen and i also  wanted to say there is a divergent in  terms of the vision  the way africa sees the fight against  climate change and the way  partners of africa will see that so  there is a disconnect there the iaea  says there needs to be a massive  increase in investment in clean energy  by 2030. how likely is that to happen do  you think  so there's a two way to look at it so if  we're looking at the  mindset that has driven us for the last  20 years or so definitely i would say  it's  very unlikely to happen however if we're  talking about the  covet spirit that has brought nation  together  and get them to spend trillions just to  make sure that they can save the planet  it's definitely possible to do it within  the next year or two i think so so  that's what i would say  and what's your message as g7 leaders  get together this week then tony  so the question would be first of all we  need to agree on what is the vision that  should be driving everybody  because as i say if there is a  disconnect and people not leaving  or not looking at the same direction i'm  more than certain that in 10 years time  we're gonna arrive but the question is  are we going to all arrive at the same  point  so the vision is important and we need  to have a vision that africa share or  emerging market share  and the vision that g7 shares or in all  of that about to save the planet  that's what i would say tony great to  hear from you thank you  you're welcome now ahead of the g7  meeting later on this week and the cop  26 summit in glasgow in november  have been renewed calls for banks to  stop financing new  coal projects as we've been hearing the  fossil fuel remains the world's  primary source of energy especially in  those developing countries  one person trying to change this is lucy  pinson whose activism successfully  pressured banks and insurance companies  in france  to stop funding new coal projects and  companies  my name is lucy panson i'm the founder  and director of reclaimed finance  i've been an activist for social justice  and against human regulation since  the end of my high school but it's when  i joined  social movements in paris in 2011 that i  really started working on  the deeper structural causes of  inequalities along the beams between  social and environmental injustice  it's really about making sure that no  more financing goes to the expansion of  the code industry  we really called all french banks to  commit to stop  providing direct financing to all  gold mines or all core plans which is  something we achieved in 2015 more or  less  when we started campaigning on code in  2013 coal wasn't such a big issue  and people didn't know in france that  coal was such a big  issue on climate and on people's life  worldwide  and we really made sure that no banks no  french banks could go  to cop21 without having  a journalist asking them how do you stop  financing cold  [Music]  we are planning some activities with our  partners  worldwide to make sure that these  financial institutions  clears our portfolio on the road to  glasgow  well that's everything from us for today  tomorrow ahead of the g7 summit in  cornwall  find out the climate impact of a cornish  pasty thanks for watching  we'll see you then

